  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read everything back from the API.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (`kibana_sample_data_flights`, id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) has 30 fields, including `Carrier`, `Cancelled`, `timestamp`, and `FlightDelayMin`. The time field is `timestamp`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-90029e56 Flights' (id `a756f129-0f5b-45c5-b37d-9b4a49842436`) has exactly two panels, both on the flights data view:\n   - a line chart of the count of flights over `timestamp`, titled \"Number of flights over time\"\n   - a pie chart of the count of flights split by `Carrier`, initially titled \"Number of flights by carrier\"\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-90029e56 Cancelled flights' (id `869a3035-446d-4b16-8f6c-030240aeda49`) is an Elasticsearch query rule (`.es-query`) that uses ES|QL. It runs every 1m over a 5m window on `timestamp`. The query is `FROM kibana_sample_data_flights | WHERE Cancelled == true | STATS cancelled_flights = COUNT(*) | WHERE cancelled_flights > 10`.\n   - My choices: the rule type rejects a non-zero threshold for ES|QL, so the \"> 10\" condition is in the query and the threshold is 0. The rule uses the `stackAlerts` consumer and has no actions. It was created enabled, so I did not need to enable it.\n\n4. **Rename:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\". Re-reading the dashboard shows two panels with the new title. The rule read back with `enabled: True`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
